Z||000000||moonie||07-26-2000||07:35 PM||||85 se 4cyl.<P>got hot twice & now leaks water into the oil & on the ground from somewhere around the water pump.<BR>fixxing to take head off & see if its warped or just maybe needs new gasket.<BR>any tip , tricks or suggestions would be greatly appreciated , since i am not very mechanicaly inclined. <BR>i can tear em up , but fixxing em is a different story.<P>thanks <BR>moonster ||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||07-26-2000||10:36 PM||||At the very least you likely need to replace both the head gasket AND HEAD BOLTS. DO NOT reuse head bolts on a 4 cyl. In fact your problem is just as likely to be the middle head bolts on the exhaust side of the head. (They were weak from the factory, and likely streatched from the heat.)<P>You'll basically need to replace all the top end gaskets. you can get them as a kit or seperately. If you get the kit you also get valve stem seals. You might as well put them in while the head is off.<P>Knowing about the weak factory bolts, I'd just replace the head gaskets and bolts. While it's posible you warped the head It's just as likely those bolts. I would have the head inspected for cracks and other problems while it's off just to be safe.<P>------------------<BR>Lake Superior? Is that what I stepped in? Sorry.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||07-26-2000||11:53 PM||||Yeah, what Ogre said, except I'd take the head into the machine shop and have them check it for straightness. Usually, just milling the head is fairly cheap. If the motor has quite a few miles on it, I might also consider having a valve job done too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||EdsB52||07-27-2000||12:14 AM||||That 4 banger is so easy to rebuild that I would just do that, depending upon current mileage / condition of the engine, and expectations you have from it in the future. I would rather remove a 4cyl engine and rebuild it than do a water pump and head while in the vehicle. Have you ever tried replacing that water hose from water pump to the right steel line? Or for that matter, all 4 main water hoses? That 4-banger is so reliable that you could probably just re-ring the engine and have the head machined for a total of 300 bucks and twenty hours. Remember, the engine comes out the bottom of the car! Don't laugh, about eight years ago I went to look at a 4-banger for sale that had the engine removed but was supposedly in good shape otherwise. When I got there, I discovered that the fool had removed the engine through the top, one piece at a time! I didn't know it was possible! You should have seen the dumbfounded look on his face when I told him that the textbook way was to remove it from the bottom. Anyway, I would rebuild it and after you do, run a wire from the radiator fan switch (located at the upper left corner of the engine) to cockpit so you can decide when the fan needs to comew on. The electric fan works killer, but the switches (electric) I've seen are set at right about boil-over time. GM = real f---'n geniuses. Good luck.||||reg||3
Z||000004||batboy||07-27-2000||12:05 PM||||Actually Ed, lots of people have pulled the 2.5 engine out of the top, without disassembling it too. Shaun's website has complete instructions how to do this.||||reg||1
Z||000005||moonie||07-27-2000||01:55 PM||||thanks Theogre , i will get new head bolts when i go to part store to get gasket set. <P>& Eds , why would i want to replace water hoses when the water is going in the oil & on the ground ? doesn't that automatically say its a head gasket or the head itself ? if it was a hose on the outside of the engine , i don't see how water could get into the oil. <P>money is a issue, i will change head gasket & bolts & have head ground if needed...tops , 100 bucks. <BR>least i can drive it to work & not have to rely on friends & family to get there. <BR>( while all the time listening to how screwed up Fiero's are , LOL ) <P>thanks for the tips fella's , happy motoring !<P>moonster<BR>||||reg||1
